{"title":"1976 IMO Problems/Problem 2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Remarks_(added_by_pf02,_December_2025)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Remarks (added by pf02, December 2025)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/8/b28e1313eb2184d873000fe53a4a1e275ad49922.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_{1}(x) = x^{2} - 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"116\" height=\"19\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/5/1e5d2a6536838586e16969ed7624d38735b533ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_{j}(x) = P_{1}(P_{j - 1}(x))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"159\" height=\"18\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/0/c20f6924610fa28a80df6356a38b8ca9f2b7e1fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j= 2,\\ldots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"70\" height=\"16\" /> Show that for any positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/4/c54e7152acd659788861f0453ac195c635e0a005.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"18\" height=\"13\" /> the roots of the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/3/943ca2e4b290bc9dbe4189a5af139ceea1d8aeb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_{n}(x) = x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"79\" height=\"18\" /> are real and distinct.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>I shall prove by induction that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/7/9f7ba371de1369bc2066fe2ef779dd977b4cbdf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_n(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" /> has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/3/6c36f0b157e8c2fbe0638c2f2b378e08d07695aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^n$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> distinct real solutions, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/8/66877bd3ddd8dd7995544baee949cc4737866732.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"37\" height=\"17\" /> are positive and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/8/66877bd3ddd8dd7995544baee949cc4737866732.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"37\" height=\"17\" /> are negative. Also, for ever root <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/0/dd09e4932cded54d7c8a865083498d5dbef70229.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|r|&lt;2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"59\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Clearly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/4/8d41f9393baffbbacc004098c08584c4a08b3db7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"18\" /> has 2 real solutions, where 1 is positive and 1 is negative. The absolute values of these two solutions are also both less than 2. This proves the base case.\n</p><p>Now assume that for some positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/2/07286a66bad883a08aaaef64004ae0e555d3df30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_k(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" /> has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/7/767b7dc4a8df95a9f8a0faa3bb155e7b49943407.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"17\" /> distinct real solutions with absolute values less than 2, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/4/8d4f26614a4233b3c4b29e6e33a1729227be3cc9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{k-1}$\" width=\"32\" height=\"15\" /> are positive and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/4/8d4f26614a4233b3c4b29e6e33a1729227be3cc9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{k-1}$\" width=\"32\" height=\"15\" /> are negative.\n</p><p>Choose a root <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/2/8d2e2d139e9de35348f0ca6e4e11737fb3cff1ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_{k+1}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/b/b9b9c1f81a3e3ee935fcf22290707079e97bcecc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1(r)=s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> is a real root of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/2/07286a66bad883a08aaaef64004ae0e555d3df30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_k(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" />. We have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/3/ae3baa56deceb7f2325dd60a698a7977e4fbac53.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-2&lt;s&lt;2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"91\" height=\"15\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/8/5c8d0292f1f795a673950f928570fed42e795c0b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0&lt;r^2&lt;4$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"82\" height=\"15\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is real and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/4/e74cfc1862c3dba607996a54e0c5f22bf14f26a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|r|&lt;2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"18\" />. Therefore all of the roots of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/a/6aa8a931efd29bc11a9118fbfd9b82e1ce51680f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_{k+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"16\" /> are real and have absolute values less than 2.\n</p><p>Note that the function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/2/8d2e2d139e9de35348f0ca6e4e11737fb3cff1ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_{k+1}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" /> is an even function, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/4/8d41f9393baffbbacc004098c08584c4a08b3db7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"18\" /> is an even function. Therefore half of the roots of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/a/6aa8a931efd29bc11a9118fbfd9b82e1ce51680f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_{k+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"16\" /> are positive, and half are negative.\n</p><p>Now assume for the sake of contradiction that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/2/8d2e2d139e9de35348f0ca6e4e11737fb3cff1ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_{k+1}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" /> has a double root <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/b/b9b9c1f81a3e3ee935fcf22290707079e97bcecc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1(r)=s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" />. Then there exists exactly one real number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/2/2523d5cb0f057c23d818960d313a54db8e8662ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r^2-2=s$\" width=\"79\" height=\"15\" />. The only way that this could happen is when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/5/b25672b843395b7d50a60e06a4199caac6e9c70a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s+2=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"72\" height=\"14\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/e/5cef753a375f985d62f1b898460b75ff85741821.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s=-2$\" width=\"55\" height=\"12\" />. However, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/f/b1fb8c280a5743865cab623bd87bf76098e51204.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|s|&lt;2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"18\" /> from our inductive hypothesis, so this is a contradiction. Therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/2/8d2e2d139e9de35348f0ca6e4e11737fb3cff1ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_{k+1}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" /> has no double roots. This proves that that the roots of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/2/8d2e2d139e9de35348f0ca6e4e11737fb3cff1ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_{k+1}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" /> are distinct.\n</p><p>This completes the inductive step, which completes the inductive proof.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/1/541595b2675b92ecbe6d337e7a0689bb905e2cdc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=2\\cos\\theta$\" width=\"82\" height=\"13\" />. We then have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/e/9feea03abf32acbac3a09b90f0ab0611fe8cf73e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1\\left(x\\right)=2\\cos2\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"128\" height=\"18\" />,\nand we can prove using induction that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/8/d18ececc20b21e44384a4c95200f2074f07f5be5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_n\\left(x\\right)=2\\cos2^n\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"138\" height=\"18\" />.\nThus, we just need to solve <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/0/bf07fe2372fcc4e60815b81fff9ef5e3a5c7b59f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\cos2^n\\theta=2\\cos\\theta$\" width=\"139\" height=\"13\" />.  This happens\nwhen <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/9/469ecdbb21e99d1630ab22208f31bd8d7b0e3587.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^n\\theta=\\theta+2\\pi k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"115\" height=\"16\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/2/252cc0a5f9e3097aae462eb962be223cd68c1b7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^n\\theta=-\\theta+2\\pi l$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"125\" height=\"16\" />. These give\nthat <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/3/6937ef8b66d5c6c5b9053417e5c55f9829cecdde.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta=\\frac{2k}{2^n-1}\\cdot\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"110\" height=\"38\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/2/2e2ef9d40ef0e8c39afd48e7d40cb07acba874d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta=\\frac{2l}{2^n+1}\\cdot\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"110\" height=\"39\" />.\n</p><p>As we can choose the range <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/6/e66a246ff00fcb19d0632bdb0aa5fe573d78a899.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0\\leq\\theta\\leq\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"77\" height=\"15\" /> to ensure no duplications,\nwe get that, upon rearranging, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/5/68502684826bc84fcdb9d72c84a3ee288522b1a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0\\leq k\\leq2^{n-1}-\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"136\" height=\"37\" /> and\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/1/c71baa4c0c33c83276b40a662a3b07bc1cf75bd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0\\leq l\\leq2^{n-1}+\\frac{1}{2}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"141\" height=\"38\" /> There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/8/66877bd3ddd8dd7995544baee949cc4737866732.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"37\" height=\"17\" /> integers in the\nfirst range and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/4/744cf3e180234633da17e531fd15ddc7af908ca0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{n-1}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"65\" height=\"16\" /> in the second. However, exactly once, they\nyield the same <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52e8ed7a3ba22130ad3984eb2cd413406475a689.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, namely <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/2/422d8671db62ffe3adc0d7dff74aa73630061d8f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta=0$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />. Thus, there are\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/f/61fb1a32cb707c5ac490631bbae56df16e7ddeb4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{n-1}+2^{n-1}+1-1=2^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"196\" height=\"16\" /> distinct values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52e8ed7a3ba22130ad3984eb2cd413406475a689.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> giving distinct\nroots <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/f/8ef4807e1c0b74e882b33c7291d44d6eab5b4b9a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 2\\cos\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"85\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/b/76bbf8b8db4cd9b33862ea30b895a8e927fd79be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_n(x) = x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"82\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Q.E.D.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Remarks_.28added_by_pf02.2C_December_2025.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Remarks_(added_by_pf02,_December_2025)\">Remarks (added by pf02, December 2025)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Remarks (added by pf02, December 2025)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/a/12a13743f0019a51abbaa321af540802715548bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />:  Solution 1 is not a solution to the problem.  It shows\nthat <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/1/0016f196b243746b46223adbf21150f513b4be4c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_n(x) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"80\" height=\"20\" /> has real, distinct roots, but the problems asked for\na proof that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/b/76bbf8b8db4cd9b33862ea30b895a8e927fd79be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_n(x) = x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"82\" height=\"20\" /> has real, distinct roots.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/9/f4974d7b088479a8eb32891d86d0b46aedb5c363.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{2}$\" width=\"9\" height=\"12\" />:  Solution 2 was in bad need of some editing.  I did this\nediting, and I believe the solution is now more readable and to the point.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/d/38d349451f157180bef169527de7d4499465377c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />:  The polynomials <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/7/9f7ba371de1369bc2066fe2ef779dd977b4cbdf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_n(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" /> from this problem are closely\nrelated to the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Chebyshev_polynomials_of_the_first_kind\" title=\"Chebyshev polynomials of the first kind\">Chebyshev polynomials of the first kind</a>. See also\n<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chebyshev_polynomials\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chebyshev_polynomials</a>.  Specificly,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/3/5c3d015645f9e848af3d301510bbc1b285027b11.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_n(x) = 2T_{2^n} \\left( \\frac{x}{2} \\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"145\" height=\"35\" />\n</p><p>One way to see this is by making the substitution <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/3/a9398fd78c32fd095ae120d80a9773e202269cd3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 2\\cos \\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"85\" height=\"14\" />,\nlike in solution 2.  Match this, and the resulting \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/8/50875785a50293d87a79a88b0b97c40169643cea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_n(x) = 2\\cos (2^n \\theta)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"149\" height=\"20\" /> to one of the definitions of Chebyshev\npolynomials, namely, the Chebyshev polynomial <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/c/38c9b08aba051c18344b0de5889b24a25c0f8b71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T_m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"22\" height=\"15\" /> is the unique\npolynomial satisfying <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/c/afc7dc23f4e76a302604000b82b0c1f0e65e0037.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T_m (\\cos \\theta) = \\cos (m\\theta)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"163\" height=\"20\" />, or\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/3/9939b5697ed21fb671489ddf991453ac6837618d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T_m(x) = \\cos (m \\arccos x).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"198\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/0/fd08f72380d01efb7467ea6cb7c741acf72bc2c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />:  The observation above suggests another solution to the\nproblem.  The idea is to look at the graphs of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/7/ca72dd6a2d542ebda141e1b6acc0ea783df46739.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = P_n(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"81\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/0/a40fee4603b4536f856dde720ee499468ace443f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"13\" />.\nWe want to prove that these two graphs intersect at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/3/6c36f0b157e8c2fbe0638c2f2b378e08d07695aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^n$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> points, and\nthere are no points where they are tangent.  Then the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-coordinates\nof the intersections are exactly the solutions to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/b/76bbf8b8db4cd9b33862ea30b895a8e927fd79be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_n(x) = x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"82\" height=\"20\" />, and they\nare real and distinct.\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Prob_1976_2.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/f/f1/Prob_1976_2.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"600\" height=\"564\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>To see that the two graphs indeed intersect at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/3/6c36f0b157e8c2fbe0638c2f2b378e08d07695aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^n$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> points, we remember\nthat the Chebyshev polynomial <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/f/e4fe51661da2eabbd80cb6c7080652f96263b19d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T_n(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"46\" height=\"20\" /> has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> real, distinct roots in\nthe interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/9/149f6a15ba033bfb273e7af037a90ffe9dbaa218.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(-1, 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"56\" height=\"20\" />, and that the values of the local maximums and\nminimums of the polynomial are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/f/7cfbd35086ffefb83637d42166582b43c8a1ff4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pm 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" />.  (We could accept these two\nfacts as reasonably well known; see section \"Roots and extrema\" from\nthe Wikipedia article I gave above.  However, one may argue that they\nare not well known, and they ought to be proven, for completeness.)\n</p><p>I will not give the details of this solution even though it provides some\ninsight, because it is more complicated than Solution 2 above, and because\nproving the facts we use about Chebyshev polynomials would use the idea\nof substituting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> with a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/5/bb5e108fdc4bf4fb6151cb922992f23bc6c35985.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos$\" width=\"24\" height=\"8\" /> function, which we are already doing\nin Solution 2.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO\" title=\"1976 IMO\">1976 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems\" title=\"1976 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1976 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">Problem 1</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1976 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1976 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1976 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1976 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1976 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1976 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017172242\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.050 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.085 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 463/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 894/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 44/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 14900/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    6.570      1 Template:IMO_box\n100.00%    6.570      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:6413:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017172242 and revision id 273524. Rendering was triggered because: a